    "code": {
      "text": "CT Angiography Chest (pulmonary embolism protocol)"
    },
    "effectiveDateTime": "2026-10-11T22:18:00",
    "issued": "2026-10-11T23:02:00",
    "basedOn": [
      {
        "display": "Kimberly Shaw, DO, Round Rock Regional Emergency Department"
      }
    ],
    "performer": [
      {
        "display": "Round Rock Regional Hospital"
      }
    ],
    "resultsInterpreter": [
      {
        "display": "Olivia Grant, MD"
      }
    ],
    "conclusion": "1. No pulmonary embolism.\n2. Dilated ascending aorta measuring 4.4 cm. Recommend annual follow-up imaging with CT angiography or MR angiography.\n3. 5 mm hepatic hypodensity, too small to characterize. In a patient without known malignancy, no follow-up imaging is needed.\n4. Mild dependent atelectasis.",
    "presentedForm": [
      {
        "contentType": "text/plain",
        "text": "EXAM:\nCT ANGIOGRAPHY CHEST (PULMONARY EMBOLISM PROTOCOL)\n\nCLINICAL INDICATION:\nAcute shortness of breath and right-sided pleuritic chest pain 2 days after a 4-hour flight. Elevated D-dimer.\n\nTECHNIQUE:\nCT angiography of the chest was performed after intravenous administration of 75 mL iohexol 350 using a pulmonary embolism protocol. Multiplanar and MIP reformats were reviewed. CTDIvol: 9.8 mGy. DLP: 352 mGy-cm.\n\nCOMPARISON:\nNone available.\n\nFINDINGS:\nPulmonary arteries: Adequate opacification of the pulmonary arteries. No filling defect to the subsegmental level. Main pulmonary artery measures 2.7 cm.\nThoracic aorta: The ascending aorta measures 4.4 cm at the level of the right pulmonary artery. No dissection or intramural hematoma. The aortic arch and descending thoracic aorta are normal in caliber.\nHeart: Heart size is normal. No pericardial effusion.\nLungs and airways: Mild dependent atelectasis in both lower lobes. No consolidation. No suspicious pulmonary nodules. Central airways are patent.\nPleura: No pleural effusion or pneumothorax.\nMediastinum and hila: No lymphadenopathy.\nUpper abdomen: 5 mm hypodensity in hepatic segment 6, too small to characterize. Otherwise unremarkable.\nBones: No acute osseous abnormality.\n\nIMPRESSION:\n1. No pulmonary embolism.\n2. Dilated ascending aorta measuring 4.4 cm. Recommend annual follow-up imaging with CT angiography or MR angiography.\n3. 5 mm hepatic hypodensity, too small to characterize. In a patient without known malignancy, no follow-up imaging is needed.\n4. Mild dependent atelectasis."
      }
    ]
  },
